/* Data packs for blocks/viewpoint-direction.html (prim:viewpoint-direction):
 * viewpoint-direction-ja, viewpoint-direction-en, viewpoint-direction-zh.
 *
 * Pack format
 *   groups     { id: {zh, en} } kinds of viewpoint form (giving/receiving, coming/going, passive). Evidence is shown
 *              per group, because doing well in one says nothing about another.
 *   items      step 1 (place yourself): [{id, kc, group, child?, situation, spots, me, arrow, side?, anchor?, noneOk?,
 *              eyeLabel?, thing?, q, forms, r?, gloss?, note, pair?}]
 *              situation  {zh, en} what is going on, in the interface language. Never contains the answer form.
 *              spots      [{id, pic, label:{zh,en}, w?}] two to four people or places in a row (w = target-language word).
 *              me         spot id where “me” (the 👁) belongs, or "none" (not there; set noneOk: true to offer it).
 *              arrow      [from, to] what moves: the thing given, the person moving, the action that affects someone.
 *              side       spot ids that count as “my side” (family, in-group) — drawn after the answer.
 *              anchor     spot id the form is anchored on when it is not “me” (English come toward the listener).
 *              eyeLabel   {zh, en} label for the eye when it marks whose side the story is told from (passive items).
 *              q          target-language sentence with ＿＿ for the gap.
 *              forms      [{t, ok?, at?, say?}] at = where the listener would think you stand if you said this (a red
 *                         dashed eye is drawn there); say = {zh, en} what the form means instead. Not shown as “wrong”
 *                         but as what the listener would understand; the first attempt is still recorded.
 *              pair       [{t, note}] hint 3: the same event from two viewpoints.
 *   shifts     step 2 (move the viewpoint): [{id, kc, group, situation, spots, arrow, me1, s1, situation2, me2, ans,
 *              q2?, forms2?, s2?, alt2?, note}] ans = change | same | both. change → choose the new form (forms2) and
 *              s2 shows the new sentence; same / both → alt2 shows the other sentence. "both" items and "same" items
 *              are the reverse half: the form does not follow the speaker's position, or two viewpoints are both fine.
 *   tellForms  step 3 (tell your own): [{t, find?, orient: to | away | neutral}] forms offered for the learner's own
 *              sentences; find = the string that must appear in the typed sentence (defaults to t). The page checks
 *              only that the form matches the arrow the learner set, that there is one toward and one away, and that
 *              the form appears in the sentence.
 *   reverse    side-card text: where the viewpoint is not where you stand.
 *   review     items a native teacher should check.
 */
